commit f77a7ae2719440026bcf778405c748116ccb2466
Author: Glen Taggart <glenmtaggart@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Jun 1 14:30:35 2026 -0700

    Stop the GA-proxy patch from eating the owner's __init_subclass__
    
    Installing the generic-alias proxy (via TypeVarValue's __set_name__ or
    @takes_alias) patches the owner's __init_subclass__. The patched version
    called super(owner, cls).__init_subclass__(), which starts the lookup
    *after* owner in the MRO and so silently skipped an __init_subclass__
    defined on the owner itself. The captured original was never invoked.
    
    Fix: when the owner defines its own __init_subclass__, run that hook (it
    propagates up via its own super(), like native Python). Only fall back to
    super(owner, cls) when the owner merely inherits the hook -- there the
    chain-walk through cls's MRO is what reaches siblings such as Generic /
    pydantic machinery that set __parameters__, which the owner's own MRO does
    not include.
    
    Add regression tests covering both proxy triggers, the inherited-hook
    cooperative chain, and an owner hook that cooperates via super().
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

